# Oobleck: a liquid you can run across
#
# Cornstarch and water is the one experiment on this shelf that is not
# chemistry. Nothing reacts. What changes is how the mixture answers being
# pushed: stir it slowly and it pours, stir it fast and the particles cannot
# get out of each other's way in time, so it resists like a solid. Stop, and
# it is a liquid again.
register lv1

# The classic mixture: about two parts cornstarch to one part water.
add v1 water 50mL
add v1 cornstarch 100g

# Gently. It flows.
stir v1 60rpm 5s

# Now fast. The same mixture, the same instant, a different answer.
stir v1 600rpm 5s

# A thin mixture does neither, however hard it is stirred — the effect needs
# the particles packed close enough to jam.
new beaker
add v2 water 200mL
add v2 cornstarch 20g
stir v2 600rpm 5s
inspect v2

# Read the ledger in v1: the starch and the water are exactly what went in.
# Nothing was made and nothing was used up; only the answer to the question
# "how hard are you pushing?" changed.
inspect v1

# What this does not model: there is no viscosity here, no yield stress and
# no critical shear rate — only the bounded statement that this mixture is
# one of the ones that does this, and that this stir was fast enough to
# notice. Particle size, starch source and how long it has stood all matter
# in a real bowl and none of them are represented.
